{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"For a U.S. equity ETF, **portfolio trading costs affect NAV and returns but generally are excluded from the reported expense ratio**:\n\n- **Brokerage commissions** are explicit transaction costs, generally added to securities’ purchase cost or deducted from sale proceeds. They reduce fund assets and performance, rather than being classified as operating expenses.\n- **Bid–ask spreads** are implicit costs embedded in execution prices. Buying at the ask and selling at the bid generally lowers NAV relative to otherwise identical, frictionless trading.\n- **Market impact**—the fund’s trades moving prices against it—similarly worsens execution and performance, without appearing as a separate expense-ratio charge.\n\nThese refer to trades **inside the portfolio**. Spreads and commissions investors pay when trading ETF shares generally affect their own returns, not fund NAV.\n\n**Worth knowing:** The expense ratio is not an all-in cost measure; in-kind creations/redemptions can reduce portfolio trading costs."}}
